lesson:
malloc gives you a block of a fixed size. But often you don't know how many items are coming: readings arrive until the sensor stops, enemies spawn until the wave ends. You need an array that **grows**. Python's list, C++'s `std::vector` and Java's `ArrayList` are all this one idea, and in C you build it yourself.

A dynamic array is three things kept together:

```
typedef struct {
    int *data;   /* the items, on the heap */
    int len;     /* how many are in use */
    int cap;     /* how many fit right now */
} IntList;
```

To add an item: if there's room (`len < cap`), put it at `data[len]` and add 1 to `len`. If it's full, make the block bigger first with **realloc**, which gives you a bigger block with the old items already copied in.

**Double the capacity, don't add 1.** Growing by one each time means copying the whole array on every push: slow for big logs. Doubling (4, 8, 16, 32...) means a million pushes need only about 20 reallocs. That's why every real dynamic array does it.

**The safe realloc pattern.** If realloc fails it returns NULL, but your old block is *still there and still yours*. So never write `l->data = realloc(l->data, ...)`: on failure that overwrites your only pointer with NULL, and the old block leaks. Use a temporary:

```
int list_push(IntList *l, int value)
{
    if (l->len == l->cap) {
        int new_cap = l->cap ? l->cap * 2 : 4;
        int *tmp = realloc(l->data, new_cap * sizeof *tmp);
        if (tmp == NULL) return 0;      /* old data still safe */
        l->data = tmp;
        l->cap = new_cap;
    }
    l->data[l->len++] = value;
    return 1;
}
```

Handy fact: `realloc(NULL, size)` acts like `malloc(size)`, so an empty list can start with `data = NULL` and no special case.

**A trap: realloc can move the block.** After a push, any pointer you kept into the old `data` may point at freed memory. Keep **indexes**, not pointers, into a growing array. (Game engines hit this constantly with lists of entities.)

**Freeing.** One `free(l->data)` frees the whole array, then reset `data = NULL, len = 0, cap = 0` so the list is safely empty again.

In this unit's files, `my_realloc` stands in for realloc so the tests can count blocks.

example.code:
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

typedef struct { int *data; int len; int cap; } IntList;

int list_push(IntList *l, int value)
{
    if (l->len == l->cap) {
        int new_cap = l->cap ? l->cap * 2 : 4;
        int *tmp = realloc(l->data, new_cap * sizeof *tmp);
        if (tmp == NULL) return 0;
        l->data = tmp;
        l->cap = new_cap;
        printf("  grew to %d\n", new_cap);
    }
    l->data[l->len++] = value;
    return 1;
}

int main(void)
{
    IntList scores = {NULL, 0, 0};
    for (int i = 1; i <= 10; i++) list_push(&scores, i * 100);
    printf("%d scores, room for %d, last is %d\n", scores.len, scores.cap, scores.data[scores.len - 1]);
    free(scores.data);
    scores.data = NULL;
    scores.len = scores.cap = 0;
    return 0;
}

